II. For the occaſionoccasion of diſpoſingdisposing goods it may be ordinary or extraordinary. Extraordinary for the good of the husband himſelfehimself, and others in the family, or ſuchsuch as are out of the family. If there fall out an extraordinary occaſionoccasion whereby the wife by diſpoſingdisposing the goods without or againſtagainst the conſentconsent of her husband may bring a great good to the family, or preuentprevent and keepekeep a great miſchiefemischief from it, ſheshe is not to ſtaystay for his conſentconsent; inſtanceinstance the example of 1 Sam. 25. 18. &etcc.

                  Abigail. Thus a faithfullfaithful prouidentprovident wife obſeruingobserving her husband to riot, and to ſpendspend all he can get in carding, dicing, and drinking, may without his conſentconsent lay vpup what goods ſheshe can for her husbands, her own, her childrens, and whole houſholdshouseholds good. This is no part of diſobediencedisobedience, but a point wherein ſheshe may ſhewshow her ſelfeself a great good helpehelp vntounto her husband; 
                     Gen. 2. 18. for which end a wife was firſtfirst made.

1. That the wife be ſuresure that God hath commanded her that which ſheshe doth without or againſtagainst her husbands conſentconsent. If ſheshe doubt, then ſheshe muſtmust ſtaystay, and for bearebearforbear till ſheshe gainegain his conſentconsent. When two oppoſiteopposite caſescases meet together, and the one be doubtfulldoubtful, the other plaineplain and expreſſeexpress: the doubtfulldoubtful caſecase muſtmust giuegive place to the more euidentevident. Now the law of ſubiectionsubjection is indefinite, thy deſiredesire ſhallshall be ſubiectsubject to thine huſbandhusband; 

